  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nPartly cloudy skies and mainly dry conditions will prevail this\nmorning before a potent H5 s/wv trof and surface cold front\nbrings an increasing chance for showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening. Despite limited daytime instability, the \ncombination of sufficient deep layer shear and inverted-V \nsoundings thanks to dry air at the surface will likely allow for\ngusty to locally damaging winds within any stronger cells. \nLatest CAM guidance indicates that north-central NH and ME are \nthe most favorable locations for showers and storms, with \nactivity beginning across the north potentially around noon \nbefore drifting southeastward through the afternoon and early \nevening. It will otherwise be a breezy day with WSW wind gusts \nup to 25 mph with high temperatures into the 70s to middle 80s \nfrom north to south. \n\nShowers and storms will wane tonight behind the cold front with\novernight low temperatures into the 40s and 50s. Patchy fog is\npossible, especially across the valleys and in areas that\nexperience earlier rainfall. \n\nHigh pressure will return on Monday with tranquil weather\nconditions. Partly to mostly sunny skies will prevail with high\ntemperatures into the 70s to near 80 degrees and lows mainly in\nthe 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA ridge builds into the Great Lakes through the week, while New \nEngland looks to remain on the eastern periphery of the ridge, and \ncloser to a deepening trough across the Canadian Maritimes. This \nbrings a more unsettled and cooler pattern, with multiple systems \nriding around the edge of the ridge.\n\nThe first system likely arrives during the day on Tuesday. Low \npressure tracks through southern Canada, while a warm front looks \nlikely to stall across New England and serve to focus more repeated \nrounds of showers and storms. At least at this point, most of this \nactivity looks to be more focused across southern New England on \nTuesday. \n\nA fresh wave of moisture on Wednesday looks to make more progress \nnorthward with the front. By this time, what's left of the low \npressure system looks to make it's closest pass to northern New \nEngland, with both features bringing the best chance for widespread \nprecip. \n\nThen by the Thursday to Friday timeframe the forecast becomes less \ncertain. It's possible that the trough consolidates more south of \nNova Scotia, allowing the ridge to begin pushing into New England \nwith warmer and drier conditions. But it's also possible the trough \nremains more elongated, keeping the unsettled conditions ongoing \ninto the beginning of Labor Day Weekend. On this evening's Euro and \nAIFS suites a slightly warmer solution was shown over the 12z runs, \nbut it's still too early to know whether or not this is a trend yet. \nWe'll continue to watch this over the coming days.",
